#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Scan a channel list on an SDR receiver, like a police/fire scanner.

  emcomm-scanner                 open the scanner window
  emcomm-scanner --list [FILE]   print the channels it would scan, as JSON lines
  emcomm-scanner --gqrx-bookmarks   print the channel list as Gqrx bookmarks
  emcomm-scanner --receivers     list the SDR receivers plugged in, as JSON lines
  emcomm-scanner --pick-receiver ask which receiver Gqrx should use (for emcomm-start)

The channel list is ~/Scanner channels.csv. The station ships Vermont's
emergency channels; replace the file (or use "Load a list" in the window) with
any CSV that has a Frequency column. Recognised columns, any order:
  Frequency (MHz), Alpha Tag or Name, Group or Tag, Agency or Description,
  County, Tone (e.g. "118.8 PL", "023 DPL", "CSQ"), Mode, Latitude, Longitude
Mode is FM or AM; left empty, the aircraft band (108-137 MHz) is AM and the
rest FM. The station's airband channels (Guard, search and rescue, air to air,
nearby airports) are added to the list as an "Airband" group unless the list
already has those frequencies; turn the group off to leave them out.
Channels with a site location are filtered by distance from the station's grid
square (Settings > User); channels without one (statewide) are always kept.

Receivers: an RTL-SDR is read through librtlsdr at 240 kS/s; anything else
SoapySDR knows (Airspy, Airspy HF+, HackRF, SDRplay, LimeSDR, bladeRF...) at
its lowest usable rate. The receiver is tuned 60 kHz below the channel so its
DC spike stays out of it, and narrow FM and AM are demodulated here with numpy.
The FM squelch is the classic noise squelch: with no carrier the demodulated audio
is loud hiss above 6 kHz, and it goes quiet as a signal comes up, so it works
the same whatever the antenna, gain or receiver. AM has no such hiss, so its
squelch compares the channel's power with the noise floor around it. Tone squelch checks the CTCSS
(PL) tone before stopping on a channel; DPL (DCS) channels stop on any carrier.
Audio plays through PipeWire (pacat).

The weather radio alert listener (emcomm-nwr) uses the first RTL-SDR. While the
scanner holds that dongle it writes ~/.local/state/emcomm/sdr.json and the
listener pauses; given another receiver, the scanner leaves the dongle alone.
The ADS-B receiver's dongle (emcomm-adsb) isn't offered at all, and the weather
radio listener's "first" is then the first of the others.
"""

